00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:21.660
If you're a company dealing
with loads of complex data,

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.060
then chances are you
deal with a whole set

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:27.370
of computational workflows in
your computing environment.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:30.570
For example, let's say you
had a lot of on-prem files

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.640
that you needed to push
up to Cloud storage.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.140
And each time you
did, you wanted

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.680
to load the data into BigQuery
and run an ETL query, which

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.450
notifies the Slack
channel when progress

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.720
is complete with a link
to the logs from the work.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.640
Now, traditionally,
creating and managing

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.070
these types of workflows
on Google Cloud Platform

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.140
was a cumbersome
operation that ranged

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.890
from easy and inflexible,
like using Cron simple tasks,

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.720
to really complex
frameworks that

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.220
might require a
team of engineers

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.020
to build and get right.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.420
Thankfully, Cloud
Composer has recently

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.310
been released to
general availability

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.220
to make all of that
a whole lot easier.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.220
At its core, Cloud Composer
is a scalable service

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:11.280
built on the open-source
project Apache Airflow,

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.920
meaning that you can still
focus on authoring Airflow

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.100
workflows while GCP
makes creating, managing,

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.590
and deploying your
workflows much easier.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:20.850
It works like this.

00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.890
Cloud Composer
orchestrates your workflow

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.880
by using Directed Acyclic
Graphs, or DAGs, which

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.130
are the representation of tasks
that Airflow needs to run,

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:31.710
including all of
the relationships

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.060
and dependencies involved.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.860
And to facilitate
this orchestration,

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.260
composers use Python
to let you dynamically

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.810
create powerful pipelines
quickly and collaborate

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.774
alongside anyone familiar
with the language.

00:01:42.774 --> 00:01:44.190
Of course, running
on Google Cloud

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.110
means that your
tasks have access

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.120
to the full suite
of Google services,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:51.030
including access to the
developer console, Cloud

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:56.140
SDK, Identity-Aware Proxy,
Stackdriver, IAM, and more.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:58.800
But one of the best things is
that Cloud Composer gives you

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.630
the ability to
connect your pipeline

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.630
through a single orchestration
tool, whether your workflow

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:08.449
lives on premises, in multiple
clouds, or fully within GCP.

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:11.940
The ability to author, schedule,
and monitor your workflows

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:13.770
in a unified manner
means that you

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:15.990
can break down the silos
in your environment

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.432
and focus less on
infrastructure.

00:02:18.432 --> 00:02:19.890
Now, in the full
talk, the speakers

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:22.320
give a great example
of the core process

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.420
of how to use
Composer, including

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.690
a live demo of getting a
workflow up and running.

